deep.reconstruction:
This page contains no mathematics. It holds pencil drafts of two personal letters, written over each other with heavy deletions.

(1) Lines 1-7 are the opening of a family letter, 'Lieber W.' (W. is quite possibly his brother Wilhelm, but that is unsure). Riemann writes that winter is ending and that he has now been 'here' about a quarter of a year. He says his resolution to write more often has gone unfulfilled, asks for indulgence, and asks that the addressee not repay him in kind ('mit Gleichem vergeltest').

(2) From 'Ew. Wohlgeboren' on is a draft to a physician, reworked several times. Riemann reports on the effect of the prescribed mixture/remedy ('verordneten Mixtur', 'angewandten Arzneimittels'):
- he began the mixture on Sunday evening;
- he repeated the dose every twelve hours as prescribed until the desired effect (apparently a bowel movement) occurred on the morning of the 3rd;
- he thanks the doctor for his advice ('Rathschläge');
- he reports on a morning symptom that has occurred irregularly since Monday;
- he asks whether the treatment should be continued.
The draft ends with closing formulas ('mit der ausgezeichnetsten Hochachtung', 'Ew. Wohlgeboren') and abbreviated signature formulas.

No derivation, formula or computation appears. For context only: the previous page continues similar letter drafts, plus an upside-down note w = 2m + 2p - 2 (a Riemann-Hurwitz-type branch-point count). The next page is a draft of §20 of the 1851 Inauguraldissertation (the mapping theorem for simply connected surfaces). This suggests the sheet was reused as scrap around the time of the dissertation, but the page itself carries no mathematical content.
